                  Drag with the mouse to rotate the surface. You can open the plot in a new page from here: <url href="https://c3d.libretexts.org/CalcPlot3D/index.html?type=z;z=y;funname=f;visible=true;umin=-2;umax=2;vmin=-2;vmax=2;grid=31;format=normal;alpha=-1;revorient=false;shownormals=false;showvf=false;numnormals=;normalscale=;normalwidth=;hidemyedges=false;constcol=rgb(255,0,0);view=0;contourcolor=red;fixdomain=false;activetrace=false;contourplot=true;showcontourplot=false;firstvalue=-1;stepsize=0.2;numlevels=11;list=;uselist=false;xnum=46;ynum=46;show2d=false;hidesurface=false;hidelabels=true;showprojections=false;surfacecontours=true;projectioncolor=rgba(255,0,0,1);showxygrid=false;showxygridonbox=false;showconstraint=false&amp;type=vector;vector=%3C-1,%201,%201%3E;label=;showlabel=false;labelposition=end;visible=true;color=rgb(255,193,7);size=2;initialpt=(0,0,0);dashed=false&amp;type=vector;vector=%3C-1,%20-1,%20-1%3E;label=v-%3E_%7B1%7D;showlabel=true;labelposition=end;visible=true;color=rgb(255,255,255);size=2;initialpt=(0,0,0);dashed=false&amp;type=window;showfunnot=false;hsrmode=0;nomidpts=true;anaglyph=-1;center=8.577426865460591,4.916907810546503,-1.5005885346290089,1;focus=0,0,0,1;up=0.29409352696871394,-0.07190374909237375,0.953068123620533,1;transparent=false;alpha=140;twoviews=false;unlinkviews=false;axisextension=0.7;shownormals=false;shownormalsatpts=false;xaxislabel=x;yaxislabel=y;zaxislabel=z;edgeson=true;faceson=true;showbox=true;showaxes=true;showticks=true;perspective=true;centerxpercent=0.5;centerypercent=0.5;rotationsteps=30;autospin=true;xygrid=false;yzgrid=false;xzgrid=false;gridsonbox=true;gridplanes=false;gridcolor=rgb(128,128,128);lastaddedsurfaceactive=true;disabletrace=false;activefun=-1;xmin=-2;xmax=2;ymin=-2;ymax=2;zmin=-2;zmax=2;xscale=1;yscale=1;zscale=1;zcmin=-4;zcmax=4;xscalefactor=1;yscalefactor=1;zscalefactor=1;tracemode=0;keep2d=false;zoom=1.02" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the plot</url>
                </p>
              </instructions>
              <static>
                <image source="/images/2025_08_04_6f11792a371c74850db7g-1.png">
                  <shortdescription>Two non-parallel vectors u and v in xyz-space and the plane they define.</shortdescription>
                  <description>
                    <p>Two non-parallel vectors <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> in <m>xyz</m>-space and the plane they define.</p>
                  </description>
                </image>
              </static>
            </interactive>
          </figure>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Formula 2 is undefined if <m>\vec{u}</m> or <m>\vec{v}</m> equal
            <m>\vec{0}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <note>
    <p>
      Any time we say <q>the angle between the vectors</q>, unless otherwise
      stated, we mean the angle that is between 0 and <m>\pi</m>
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-ch01-03-note-3-1">
      <caption>This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to Note 3.1.</caption>
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch01-03-note-3-1" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="hpfn5up5tn">
        <instructions>
          <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here: <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/hpfn5up5tn" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
        </instructions>
        <static>
          <image source="/images/Note-3.1.png">
            <shortdescription>Two vectors and their interior and exterior angles.</shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>Two vectors and their interior and exterior angles.</p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </static>
      </interactive>
    </figure>
  </note>

  <example xml:id="example-ch01-03-ex-3-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute the following:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\langle 0, 1\rangle \cdot\langle 1, 1\rangle</m>, using
              formula 1 .
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\langle1, 1\rangle \cdot\langle 0, 1\rangle</m>, using
              formula 2 (you may assume the angle between the vectors is
              <m>\frac{\pi}{4}</m> ).
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Formula 1 multiplies the corresponding components and adds the
        results:
        <md>
          \langle 0,1 \rangle \cdot \langle 1,1 \rangle = 0(1)+1(1) = 1
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The magnitudes are
        <m>|\langle 1,1 \rangle| = \sqrt{1^2+1^2} = \sqrt{2}</m> and
        <m>|\langle 0,1 \rangle| = \sqrt{0^2+1^2} = 1</m>, so formula 2 gives
        <md>
          \langle 1,1 \rangle \cdot \langle 0,1 \rangle = \sqrt{2} \cdot 1 \cdot \cos\left( \frac{\pi}{4} \right) = \sqrt{2} \cdot \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} = 1
        </md>
        which agrees with the result of item 1: the two formulas are
        equivalent, and the dot product does not depend on the order of the
        vectors.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="example-ch01-03-ex-3-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the angle between <m>\vec{u}=\langle 2,1,2\rangle</m> and
        <m>\vec{v}=\langle 3,-1,1\rangle</m>. What happens if we use the dot
        product to find the angle between <m>\vec{u}=\langle 1,0\rangle</m>
        and <m>\vec{v}=\langle 0,-1\rangle</m> ?
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Since both vectors are nonzero, we can solve formula 2 for
        <m>\cos(\theta)</m>, computing the dot product with formula 1:
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\vec{u} \cdot \vec{v} = 2(3)+1(-1)+2(1) = 7, \quad |\vec{u}| = \sqrt{4+1+4} = 3, \quad |\vec{v}| = \sqrt{9+1+1} = \sqrt{11}</mrow>
          <mrow>\cos(\theta) = \frac{\vec{u} \cdot \vec{v}}{|\vec{u}||\vec{v}|} = \frac{7}{3\sqrt{11}} \Rightarrow \theta = \arccos\left( \frac{7}{3\sqrt{11}} \right)</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        For <m>\vec{u}=\langle 1,0\rangle</m> and
        <m>\vec{v}=\langle 0,-1\rangle</m> the dot product is
        <m>\vec{u} \cdot \vec{v} = 1(0)+0(-1) = 0</m>, so
        <md>
          \cos(\theta) = \frac{0}{1 \cdot 1} = 0 \Rightarrow \theta = \frac{\pi}{2}
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        We get a right angle: even though <m>\vec{v}</m> points along the
        negative <m>y</m>-axis, the angle between the two vectors that lies
        between 0 and <m>\pi</m> is <m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m>, so the vectors are
        perpendicular.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <definition>
    <title>Orthogonality</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Two vectors <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> in 2D or 3D are
        orthogonal if <m>\vec{u} \cdot \vec{v}=0</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <remark>
    <p>
      Orthogonal is another word for perpendicular (as defined in geometry).
      In 3D, the vectors are perpendicular in the plane they define.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-ch01-03-orthogonal-vectors">
      <caption>The vectors <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> are orthogonal</caption>
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch01-03-orthogonal-vectors" variant="controls" width="90%" aspect="3:2"
