            <description>
              <p>Two orthogonal vectors <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> meeting at a right angle. On the background the plane perpendicular to <m>\vec{v}</m> that contains <m>\vec{u}</m> passing from the origin of the axes.</p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </static>
      </interactive>
    </figure>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch01-03-ex-3-3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine whether the vectors <m>\vec{u}=\langle 5,-2\rangle</m> and
        <m>\vec{v}=\langle 1,5/2\rangle</m> are perpendicular using the dot
        product.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        We compute the dot product using formula 1:
        <md>
          \vec{u} \cdot \vec{v} = 5(1)+(-2)\left( \frac{5}{2} \right) = 5-5 = 0
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Since <m>\vec{u} \cdot \vec{v} = 0</m>, the vectors are orthogonal,
        i.e. perpendicular.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <title>Orthogonal Projections</title>

    <p>
      The dot product allows us define projections. Think of projections as
      squishing (casting a shadow of) one vector into another.
    </p>
  </note>

  <definition>
    <title>Orthogonal Projections</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> be two vectors (in 2D or 3D),
        with <m>\vec{v} \neq 0</m>. The orthogonal projection (or simply
        <q>projection</q>) of <m>\vec{u}</m> into <m>\vec{v}</m> is given by
        <md>
          \operatorname{proj}_{\vec{v}} \vec{u}=\left(\frac{\vec{u} \cdot \vec{v}}{\vec{v} \cdot \vec{v}}\right) \vec{v}
        </md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch01-03-projection-cases">
    <caption>The orthogonal projection of <m>\vec{u}</m> onto <m>\vec{v}</m> for the two possible ranges of the angle <m>\theta</m> between them.</caption>

    <sidebyside widths="45% 45%" margins="auto" valign="middle">
      <figure xml:id="fig-ch01-03-projection-cases-a">
        <caption><m>0\leq \theta \lt \frac{\pi}{2}</m>. This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_08_04_6f11792a371c74850db7g-5.</caption>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch01-03-projection-cases-a" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="d6jcpar33o">
          <!--<instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here: <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>-->

          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_08_04_6f11792a371c74850db7g-5.png">
              <shortdescription>The orthogonal projection of u onto v when the angle theta between them is at least 0 and less than pi over 2.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The orthogonal projection of <m>\vec{u}</m> onto <m>\vec{v}</m> when the angle <m>\theta</m> between them is at least 0 and less than <m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m>.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch01-03-projection-cases-b">
        <caption><m>\frac{\pi}{2} \lt \theta \leq \pi</m>. This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_08_04_6f11792a371c74850db7g-5-1.</caption>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch01-03-projection-cases-b" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="bhkhtavyvj">
          <!--<instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here: <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>-->

          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_08_04_6f11792a371c74850db7g-5-1.png">
              <shortdescription>The orthogonal projection of u onto v when the angle theta between them is greater than pi over 2 and at most pi.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The orthogonal projection of <m>\vec{u}</m> onto <m>\vec{v}</m> when the angle <m>\theta</m> between them is greater than <m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m> and at most <m>\pi</m>.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </sidebyside>
  </figure>
  <p>
     You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:<url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/hpfn5up5tn" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the left graph</url>, <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/bhkhtavyvj" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the right graph</url>
  </p>
  <!--\caption*{From openstax, Calculus Volume 3, Section 2.2}-->
  <!--\includegraphics[max width=0.7\textwidth]{2025_08_04_6f11792a371c74850db7g-5}-->

  <remark>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            When we say that we are projecting <m>\vec{u}</m> into
            <m>\vec{v}</m>, we are actually projecting <m>\vec{u}</m> into
            the line containing <m>\vec{v}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            As seen in the pictures, <m>\operatorname{proj}_{\vec{v}} \vec{u}</m>
            is a vector. Specifically, a scalar multiple of <m>\vec{v}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Important: As seen in the pictures, if the angle <m>\theta</m>
            between <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> satisfies
            <m>0 \leq \theta \lt \frac{\pi}{2}, \operatorname{proj}_{\vec{v}} \vec{u}</m>
            is in the direction of <m>\vec{v}</m>. If
            <m>\frac{\pi}{2} \lt \theta \leq \pi, \operatorname{proj}_{\vec{v}} \vec{u}</m>
            is in the direction opposite of <m>\vec{v}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            What happens if the angle between <m>\vec{u}</m> and
            <m>\vec{v}</m> is <m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m> or <m>\vec{u}=\vec{0}</m> ?
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch01-03-ex-3-4">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Given <m>\vec{u}=\langle1,2,3\rangle</m> and
        <m>\vec{v}=\langle -3,-2,-1\rangle</m>, compute
        <m>\operatorname{proj}_{\vec{v}} \vec{u}</m>. Are <m>\vec{v}</m> and
        proj <m>_{\vec{v}} \vec{u}</m> parallel? Justify your answer.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        We compute the two dot products in the projection formula:
        <md>
          \vec{u} \cdot \vec{v} = 1(-3)+2(-2)+3(-1) = -10, \quad \vec{v} \cdot \vec{v} = (-3)^2+(-2)^2+(-1)^2 = 14
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Hence
        <md>
          \operatorname{proj}_{\vec{v}} \vec{u} = \left( \frac{\vec{u} \cdot \vec{v}}{\vec{v} \cdot \vec{v}} \right) \vec{v} = -\frac{10}{14}\langle -3,-2,-1 \rangle = -\frac{5}{7}\langle -3,-2,-1 \rangle = \left\langle \frac{15}{7}, \frac{10}{7}, \frac{5}{7} \right\rangle
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Yes, they are parallel:
        <m>\operatorname{proj}_{\vec{v}} \vec{u} = -\frac{5}{7}\vec{v}</m> is a
        scalar multiple of <m>\vec{v}</m>, so the two vectors lie on the same
        line. Since the scalar <m>-\frac{5}{7}</m> is negative,
        <m>\operatorname{proj}_{\vec{v}} \vec{u}</m> points in the direction
        opposite of <m>\vec{v}</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <p>
      Given any nonzero vector <m>\vec{v}</m>, we have that
      <md>
        \vec{v} \cdot \vec{v}=v_{1}^{2}+v_{2}^{2}=|\vec{v}|^{2}
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      We can use this fact to re-write the projection formula as follows:
      <md>
        <mrow>\operatorname{proj}_{\vec{v}} \vec{u} \amp =\left(\frac{\vec{u} \cdot \vec{v}}{\vec{v} \cdot \vec{v}}\right) \vec{v}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp =\left(\frac{\vec{u} \cdot \vec{v}}{|\vec{v}|^{2}}\right) \vec{v}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp =\left(\frac{\vec{u} \cdot \vec{v}}{|\vec{v}|}\right) \frac{\vec{v}}{|\vec{v}|}</mrow>
      </md>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <definition>
    <title>Scalar Component in the Direction of a Vector</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> be two vectors with
        <m>\vec{v} \neq \vec{0}</m>. The scalar component of <m>\vec{u}</m>
        in the direction of <m>\vec{v}</m> is given by
        <md>
          \operatorname{scal}_{\vec{v}} \vec{u}=\frac{\vec{u} \cdot \vec{v}}{|\vec{v}|}
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Moreover, we have that
        <md>
          \left|\operatorname{scal}_{\vec{v}} \vec{u}\right|=\left|\operatorname{proj}_{\vec{v}} \vec{u}\right|
        </md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <remark>
    <title>The importance of the quantity scal <m>{ }_{\vec{v}} \vec{u}</m></title>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\left|\operatorname{scal}_{\vec{v}} \vec{u}\right|</m> is a
            shortcut to compute
            <m>\left|\operatorname{proj}_{\vec{v}} \vec{u}\right|</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            From Note 6, if <m>\vec{u} \cdot \vec{v} \gt 0</m>, then
            <m>\operatorname{scal}_{\vec{v}} \vec{u} \gt 0</m>, and hence
            <m>\operatorname{proj}_{\vec{v}} \vec{u}</m> is in the direction
            of <m>\vec{v}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            If <m>\vec{u} \cdot \vec{v} \lt 0</m>, then
            <m>\operatorname{proj}_{\vec{v}} \vec{u}</m> is in the direction
            opposite of <m>\vec{v}</m> .
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch01-03-ex-3-5">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Given <m>\vec{u}=\langle 4,3\rangle, \vec{v}=\langle 5,12\rangle</m>,
        and <m>\vec{w}=\langle-4,-3\rangle</m>, find
        <m>\operatorname{scal}_{\vec{v}} \vec{u}</m> and
        <m>\operatorname{scal}_{\vec{v}} \vec{w}</m>. Moreover, determine
        whether <m>\operatorname{proj}_{\vec{v}} \vec{u}</m> and
        <m>\operatorname{proj}_{\vec{v}} \vec{w}</m> are in the direction of
        <m>\vec{v}</m> or opposite to it.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch01-03-ex-3-5">
        <caption>This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to Example 3.5.</caption>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch01-03-ex-3-5" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="ojxbsovmu1">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here: <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/ojxbsovmu1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/Example-3.5.png">
              <shortdescription>Vectors u, v, and w with their projections onto v shown in the xy-plane.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>Vectors <m>\vec{u}</m>, <m>\vec{v}</m>, and <m>\vec{w}</m> with their projections onto <m>\vec{v}</m> shown in the <m>xy</m>-plane.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        First, <m>|\vec{v}| = \sqrt{5^2+12^2} = \sqrt{169} = 13</m>. Then
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\operatorname{scal}_{\vec{v}} \vec{u} = \frac{\vec{u} \cdot \vec{v}}{|\vec{v}|} = \frac{4(5)+3(12)}{13} = \frac{56}{13}</mrow>
          <mrow>\operatorname{scal}_{\vec{v}} \vec{w} = \frac{\vec{w} \cdot \vec{v}}{|\vec{v}|} = \frac{-4(5)+(-3)(12)}{13} = -\frac{56}{13}</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Since <m>\operatorname{scal}_{\vec{v}} \vec{u} = \frac{56}{13} \gt 0</m>,
        the projection <m>\operatorname{proj}_{\vec{v}} \vec{u}</m> is in the
        direction of <m>\vec{v}</m>; since
        <m>\operatorname{scal}_{\vec{v}} \vec{w} = -\frac{56}{13} \lt 0</m>, the
        projection <m>\operatorname{proj}_{\vec{v}} \vec{w}</m> is in the
        direction opposite of <m>\vec{v}</m>, as the picture above shows.
        (Note that <m>\vec{w} = -\vec{u}</m>, which is why the two scalar
        components are negatives of each other.)
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <title>Summary</title>

    <p>
      Given two vectors <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> with
      <m>\vec{v} \neq 0</m>; and <m>0 \leq \theta \leq \pi</m> the angle
      between <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m>, then:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            If <m>0 \leq \theta \lt \frac{\pi}{2}</m> or
            <m>\vec{u} \cdot \vec{v} \gt 0 \Rightarrow \operatorname{proj}_{\vec{v}} \vec{u}</m>
            is in the direction of <m>\vec{v}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            If <m>\frac{\pi}{2} \lt \theta \leq \pi</m> or
            <m>\vec{u} \cdot \vec{v} \lt 0 \Rightarrow \operatorname{proj}_{\vec{v}} \vec{u}</m>
            is in the direction opposite of <m>\vec{v}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            If <m>\theta=\frac{\pi}{2}</m> or
            <m>\vec{u}=0 \Rightarrow \operatorname{proj}_{\vec{v}} \vec{u}=0</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            A shortcut to find
            <m>\left|\operatorname{proj}_{\vec{v}} \vec{u}\right|</m> is to
            compute it as <m>\left|\operatorname{scal}_{\vec{v}} \vec{u}\right|</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </note>

</section>
